## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement EDF scheduling, bounded queues, per-producer fairness, backpressure, and safe shutdown under load.

## Key Concepts
- Deadline-aware scheduling (EDF)
- Bounded queues with per-producer rate limits
- Backpressure signaling and cancellation
- Graceful shutdown with in-flight tracking
- Correctness under contention and timing jitter

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal EDF scheduler (Node.js)
class Task {
  constructor(id, deadline, payload){ this.id=id; this.deadline=deadline; this.payload=payload; this.cancelled=false; }
}
class EDFScheduler {
  constructor(concurrency=2){ this.queue=[]; this.concurrency=concurrency; this.active=0; this.running=true; }
  enqueue(t){ this.queue.push(t); this.queue.sort((a,b)=>a.deadline-b.deadline); }
  async nextTask(){ while(this.running){ if(this.queue.length){ const t=this.queue.shift(); if(!t.cancelled) return t; continue; } await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1)); } return null; }
  async startWorker(){ if(this.active>=this.concurrency) return; this.active++; (async ()=>{ while(this.running){ const t=await this.nextTask(); if(!t) break; await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,10)); console.log(`done ${t.id}`); } this.active--; })(); }
  shutdown(){ this.running=false; }
}
(async ()=>{ const s=new EDFScheduler(2); const now=Date.now(); s.enqueue(new Task(1, now+200, {})); s.enqueue(new Task(2, now+100, {})); s.enqueue(new Task(3, now+150, {})); s.startWorker(); s.startWorker(); setTimeout(()=>s.shutdown(),500);})();
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a realistic concurrency pattern involving partitioned streams with local ordering guarantees, bounded buffers, and backpressure mechanisms. It also evaluates understanding of cross-partition fairness and practical testing strategies for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts

- Partitioned concurrency and data locality
- Bounded buffers and backpressure propagation
- In-order processing within partitions
- Cross-partition fairness and starvation prevention
- Realistic unit and integration testing approaches

## Code Example

```go
package main

type Event struct {
    ID      string
    ShardID int
    Data    []byte
}

type ShardProcessor struct {
    queue   chan Event
    worker  func(Event)
    done    chan struct{}
}

type ConcurrentProcessor struct {
    shards    []*ShardProcessor
    shardCount int
}

func NewConcurrentProcessor(shardCount int, queueSize int, worker func(Event)) *ConcurrentProcessor {
    processor := &ConcurrentProcessor{
        shards:     make([]*ShardProcessor, shardCount),
        shardCount: shardCount,
    }

    for i := 0; i < shardCount; i++ {
        processor.shards[i] = &ShardProcessor{
            queue:  make(chan Event, queueSize),
            worker: worker,
            done:   make(chan struct{}),
        }
        go processor.shards[i].process()
    }

    return processor
}

func (p *ConcurrentProcessor) getShardID(eventID string) int {
    hash := fnv.New32a()
    hash.Write([]byte(eventID))
    return int(hash.Sum32()) % p.shardCount
}

func (p *ConcurrentProcessor) Process(event Event) error {
    shardID := p.getShardID(event.ID)
    event.ShardID = shardID

    select {
    case p.shards[shardID].queue <- event:
        return nil
    default:
        return errors.New("shard queue full - backpressure applied")
    }
}

func (s *ShardProcessor) process() {
    for {
        select {
        case event := <-s.queue:
            s.worker(event)
        case <-s.done:
            return
        }
    }
}

func (p *ConcurrentProcessor) Close() {
    for _, shard := range p.shards {
        close(shard.done)
    }
}
```

## Testing Strategy

### Unit Tests
- Verify per-shard ordering with sequential events
- Test backpressure when queues reach capacity
- Validate consistent hashing distributes events evenly

### Integration Tests
- Simulate multiple producers with varying rates
- Monitor queue depths and processing latency
- Verify no shard starvation under uneven load

### Property-Based Tests
- Generate random event streams and verify invariants
- Test edge cases: empty queues, rapid producer bursts, worker failures

